    "blogtextliquid_LBJgtB": {
      "type": "editorial--blog-text-liquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>For decades, department store fragrance halls strictly divided humanity into two aisles: pink bottles full of sugar and jasmine on the left, and dark glass bottles full of cedarwood and pepper on the right. If you were a woman, you were supposed to smell like flowers; if you were a man, you were supposed to smell like an axe-wielding lumberjack.</p><p>Today, that rigid division has completely collapsed across the UK. Unisex perfumes are now the fastest-growing and most searched category in fine fragrance, driven by shoppers who care about olfactory brilliance rather than demographic labels.</p>"
      }
    },
